WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.880
Hamlet has been having delusions, hallucinations,
anhedonia, lack of motivation, and poor speech.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.959
He sees ghosts, and all of Denmark thinks
he’s crazy.

00:00:06.959 --> 00:00:08.850
But, what is actually happening?

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.610
You, being the smarty-pants that you are,
diagnose him with schizophrenia.

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:16.650
Most people believe that schizophrenia is
caused by abnormal dopamine activity in the

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:17.650
brain.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.320
Dopamine is a neurotransmitter that is expressed
in the central nervous system and regulates

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.630
things like motivation, cognition, emotions,
and the reward system.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:29.720
Dopamine binds to dopamine receptors, which
are G-protein coupled receptors that are located

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.330
in the postsynaptic membrane.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:35.350
This changes the structure of the receptor,
which can then activate a nearby G-protein

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:39.510
by exchanging the bounded GDP molecule for
a GTP molecule.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:44.640
Once activated, the G-protein splits, with
its alpha subunit and the bound GTP moving

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.610
to bind to adenylyl cyclase.

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:51.479
The adenylyl cyclase is then activated, producing
cyclic AMP and spreading the signal throughout

00:00:51.479 --> 00:00:52.659
the cell.

00:00:52.659 --> 00:00:56.600
Schizophrenic patients have abnormal activity
at dopamine receptors, specifically D2 receptors,

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:00.440
with increased dopamine levels in the mesolimbic
area of the brain and decreased levels in

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.039
the prefrontal cortex.

00:01:02.039 --> 00:01:05.549
Increased levels cause positive symptoms,
like hallucinations and delusions, while decreased

00:01:05.549 --> 00:01:10.329
levels cause negative and cognitive symptoms,
like lack of motivation and decreased levels

00:01:10.329 --> 00:01:11.329
of emotional expression.

00:01:11.329 --> 00:01:15.159
Because the true cause of schizophrenia is
unclear, treatment aims to reduce symptoms

00:01:15.159 --> 00:01:16.689
instead.

00:01:16.689 --> 00:01:20.920
Antipsychotic drugs, specifically Risperidone,
binds to neurotransmitters like the D2 receptor

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:24.720
and inhibits dopamine from binding, which
alleviates positive, negative, and cognitive

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:25.720
symptoms.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.020
However, treatment doesn’t always work.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.250
Some are unresponsive to medication, most
drugs only reduce positive symptoms, not negative

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.990
or cognitive ones, and antipsychotics have
severe side effects.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:38.770
And in general, schizophrenia prevents people
from leading normal lives.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:42.580
They can’t form relationships or get an
education or job, and need constant support

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:43.770
to manage symptoms.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:47.240
Therefore, social awareness is needed to help
those with schizophrenia.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.530
Understanding schizophrenia can support patients
(especially ones with suicidal or unhealthy

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:56.289
lives), decrease rehospitalization and stigmatization,
monitor treatments, and bring them closer

00:01:56.289 --> 00:01:57.289
to normal life.

00:01:57.289 --> 00:01:58.289
And there we go!

00:01:58.289 --> 00:02:00.530
How can you help Hamlet and others like him?

